<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Oriah cross-service logback config. Lives in oriah-observability-starter
    so every Spring Boot module picks up the same JSON layout the moment it
    declares the dependency.

    Each log event is rendered as a one-line JSON object including:
      - ISO-8601 UTC timestamp
      - level / logger name / thread name / message
      - all MDC entries, notably traceId + spanId injected by Micrometer
        Tracing's MDC binder, so a Loki query like {service="checkout"} |~
        "traceId=abc123" lights up the full call path
      - stack trace (root-cause-first, truncated at 2KB to keep lines parseable)
      - a fixed "service" field bound to spring.application.name so the
        Promtail label and Loki query language stay simple

    A logback-spring.xml in a consumer module's own resources would still
    take precedence (classpath ordering): we don't break anyone's local
    override, we just give a working default.
-->
<configuration>

    <!--
        spring.application.name isn't known until Spring's environment is
        bound, so we read it via Spring's LogbackInitializer hook into a
        Logback context-scoped property.
    -->
    <springProperty scope="context" name="appName" source="spring.application.name" defaultValue="unknown"/>

    <appender name="JSON_CONSOLE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
        <encoder class="net.logstash.logback.encoder.LoggingEventCompositeJsonEncoder">
            <providers>
                <timestamp>
                    <timeZone>UTC</timeZone>
                    <pattern>yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSS'Z'</pattern>
                </timestamp>
                <version/>
                <logLevel/>
                <loggerName/>
                <threadName/>
                <message/>
                <mdc/>
                <stackTrace>
                    <throwableConverter class="net.logstash.logback.stacktrace.ShortenedThrowableConverter">
                        <maxDepthPerThrowable>30</maxDepthPerThrowable>
                        <maxLength>2048</maxLength>
                        <rootCauseFirst>true</rootCauseFirst>
                    </throwableConverter>
                </stackTrace>
                <pattern>
                    <pattern>{"service":"${appName:-unknown}"}</pattern>
                </pattern>
            </providers>
        </encoder>
    </appender>

    <root level="INFO">
        <appender-ref ref="JSON_CONSOLE"/>
    </root>

    <!--
        Default per-package levels. Service-specific levels still come from
        the Config Server (e.g., logging.level.com.oriah.catalog: DEBUG).
    -->
    <logger name="com.oriah" level="INFO"/>
    <logger name="org.springframework.cloud.stream" level="INFO"/>
    <logger name="org.apache.kafka" level="WARN"/>

    <!--
        ONE class raised to ERROR, and only after the cause was chased down
        and partly removed. It is not a blanket Kafka silence: the line above
        keeps org.apache.kafka at WARN, so a real consumer failure stays loud.

        The noise: Spring Cloud Stream's KafkaBinderMetrics builds its OWN
        KafkaConsumer to compute group lag. That consumer joins no group and
        owns no partitions, so every evaluation asks for offsets it is not
        assigned and this class logs, twice,

          Not updating high watermark for partition X as it is no longer
          assigned

        It was 79% of all idle log volume. The evidence it is harmless: the
        group member is consumer-<svc>-2 while the WARN comes from
        consumer-<svc>-3; every group is Stable / 1 member / LAG 0; and the
        Kafka-driven paths pass end to end (smoke steps 8 and 10).

        Why ERROR rather than fixing it outright: setting
        spring.cloud.stream.kafka.binder.metrics.default-offset-lag-metrics-enabled=false
        does NOT switch the observer off. Measured before and after, it drops
        from 12 lines/min per service to 2 — the flag moves lag computation
        from per-scrape to a cached 60s refresh, so the partition-less consumer
        still fetches, just six times less often. There is no supported switch
        that removes it, so the residue is silenced here.

        Why this cannot hide actionable degradation: OffsetFetcherUtils logs
        exactly one WARN, this one, about a partition the CALLER does not own.
        A consumer that genuinely falls behind shows up as lag on
        kafka_consumer_fetch_manager_records_lag_max (the metric the Grafana
        panel plots, asserted still present by smoke step 19), and a consumer
        that genuinely fails logs from the coordinator and listener-container
        loggers, both still at WARN.
    -->
    <logger name="org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.internals.OffsetFetcherUtils" level="ERROR"/>
</configuration>
